{"name":"Satin Souffle","hex":"#efe0bc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"satin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth woven from silk, nylon or polyester with a glossy surface and a dull back. (The same weaving technique applied to cotton produces cloth termed sateen).","Gin (the drink)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Semigloss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/satin"},{"term":"souffle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of soufflé (“dish made with eggs”)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative spelling of soufflé."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/souffle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Satin","blocks":[{"text":"A satin weave is a type of fabric weave that produces a characteristically glossy, smooth or lustrous material, typically with a glossy top surface and a dull back; it is not durable, as it tends to snag. It is one of three fundamental types of textile weaves alongside plain weave and twill weave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg/330px-Satin_weave_in_silk_90.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satin"},{"title":"Soufflé","blocks":[{"text":"A soufflé is a baked egg dish originating in France in the early 18th century. Combined with various other ingredients, it can be served as a savoury main dish or sweetened as a dessert. The word soufflé is the past participle of the French verb souffler, which means to blow, breathe, inflate or puff."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Bacon_and_Cheddar_Cheese_Souffl%C3%A9.jpg/330px-Bacon_and_Cheddar_Cheese_Souffl%C3%A9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Souffl%C3%A9"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Satin_bedding.jpg/500px